A||N||7||bHooper||Time to start debbuging a few things.||||6||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-14-2000||02:43 PM||||Now that my car is together and running (still not on the street, Yet), I have a couple more questions.<P>1) The oil pressure is awfull high!  It is around 75, shouldnt it be more like 55 or 60?  What corrects this? (w/o a major rebuild)<P>2) The idle is around 2300-2500 RPM's.  Is this just because th car has been disconected for so long?  I know there are a plethora of vacume related posibilities, but I dont see this as a majjor reason (all the tubes look OK).<P>3) If it is timeing related, how do I check that?  <P>The car seems to run just like my friends GT, except for the idle and the oil pressure.  If it were a timeing problem, I think it would sound a lot different.<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||6
Z||000001||terryk||05-14-2000||02:54 PM||||It might run that high. Idle is what counts most. What is it then?<P>Hi idle is almost always a vaccum leak. EGR tube, vacuum lines. Make sure the throttle is not hanging up too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||05-14-2000||03:14 PM||||btw, i am not getting any codes.  that has to be a good sign.  <P>where is the IAC on the v-6?<P>any thoughts on the oil pressure?<P>thanks, hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||05-14-2000||03:33 PM||||IAC is on the right side of the throttle body.  It's the "big" one on the bottom.  I believe it takes a 1 & 1/4 wrench.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||05-14-2000||03:59 PM||||I just went through this yesterday on my 2M4, one of the vacuum hoses had a small tear in it, enough to cause code 35s to get generated at idle when the engine was warm. Replacing the hose and resetting the ECM cured the problem. It was a PITA finding the leak. <P>Shut off the engine, disconnect the wire harness to the IAC valve and restart the car.  If idle goes up measurably than your IAC valve is probably OK and you should look again for a vacuum leak.  If idle doesn't change suspect the IAC valve or somewhere along the wiring harness.  I think a new IAC valve is somewhere around $50-60.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||05-14-2000||04:06 PM||||High oil pressure: the oil sending unit.<P>My oil pressure guage reads 80+ when cold, maybe drops to 60 at idle when warm.  I've replace the oil sending unit 3X. Its a fix for a couple of years. When the oil pressure remains at 80+ or higher I'll change it again.<P>High idle:  Haynes manual says Emission control systems.  All those miserable sensors, or vacuum leaks.||||reg||3
Z||000006||batboy||05-14-2000||04:32 PM||||A number a things could cause a high idle, including vacuum leaks. I have also seen in a number of past post that when the ECM has been reset, sometimes the idle is high until the car has been driven for a while. Apparently, it takes a while for the ECM to reprogram itself. I won't worry about the oil pressure. It can be a little high 70-80 PSI when the car is first started, especially if the idle is that high. If the sender is ok, chances are that it will settle down to about 60 PSI after it's fully warmed up and when it's idling at the normal 900 (or so) RPM. I'd be more concerned if the pressure was really low.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||05-14-2000||08:52 PM||||Thanks, I will try to look at these things tommorow.  As for the oil pressure and reset, that will hopefully come later in the week.  That is when I am hopeing to start driveing it around the block.  For "testing", although it may be a while b4 I get it up to the 40 mph... it will definately need bumbers and fenders to do that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">... may post some pics later tonight.<P>I will look at the oil sending unit.  Again, when a car has set for so long, one should expect some problems.<P>||||reg||1
